Lab: Run Applications as Containers and Pods
Objective
In this lab you will:
•	Deploy a pod from a container image
•	Check pod status and events
•	Troubleshoot a failed pod
•	Edit pod configuration
•	Copy debugging files to a pod
•	Use port forwarding to access a web application
The application uses PHP running on Apache HTTP Server.
________________________________________
1. Start Lab Environment
Prepare the environment.
lab start pods-review
This command ensures:
•	cluster is running
•	required files exist
________________________________________
2. Login to OpenShift
Login as developer user.
oc login -u developer -p developer \
https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
________________________________________
3. Select Project
Use the project for the lab.
oc project pods-review
________________________________________
4. Deploy Web Application Pod
Create a pod named webphp.
oc run webphp \
--image=registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/webphp:v1
Pod created:
pod/webphp created
________________________________________
5. Check Pod Status
Check status.
oc get pods
Output:
NAME     READY   STATUS
webphp   0/1     CrashLoopBackOff
Later:
STATUS: Error
Meaning:
Container keeps crashing
________________________________________
6. Check Cluster Events
View cluster events.
oc get events
Important messages:
Pulled image successfully
Started container
Back-off restarting failed container
Meaning:
Image pulled successfully
But container crashes after start
________________________________________
7. Check Pod Logs
View container logs.
oc logs webphp
Important error:
ERROR: unable to bind listening socket
Permission denied
Also:
Error retrieving pid file run/httpd.pid
Problem identified:
Permission issue in /run directory
________________________________________
8. Create Debug Pod
Use debug mode to inspect filesystem.
oc debug pod/webphp
Shell opens:
sh-4.4$
________________________________________
9. Inspect /run Directory
List directory permissions.
ls -la /run
Output example:
drwx--x---  root apache httpd
Meaning:
Directory permissions do not allow container user access
________________________________________
10. Check Container User
Check user identity.
id
Example:
uid=1000770000
gid=0(root)
Meaning:
•	container runs as random UID
•	belongs to root group
This is a security feature in Kubernetes and Red Hat OpenShift.
Problem:
Root group lacks permissions
Exit debug pod.
exit
________________________________________
11. Fix the Application Image
Developer released a fixed container image:
webphp:v2
Edit the pod.
oc edit pod/webphp
Change image:
image: registry.ocp4.example.com:8443/redhattraining/webphp:v2
Save and exit.
________________________________________
12. Verify Pod is Running
Check status again.
oc get pods
Output:
NAME     READY   STATUS
webphp   1/1     Running
________________________________________
13. Confirm Container User
Check user again.
oc exec -it webphp -- id
Example:
uid=1000680000
gid=0(root)
Still:
Unprivileged user
________________________________________
14. Check Correct Permissions
Check /run directory.
oc exec -it webphp -- ls -la /run
Correct permissions:
drwxrwx--- root root httpd
Meaning:
Root group now has write permission
Application works correctly.
________________________________________
15. Setup Port Forwarding
Forward local port to pod.
oc port-forward pod/webphp 8080:8080
Output:
127.0.0.1:8080 → pod:8080
Keep this terminal open.
________________________________________
16. Test Web Application
Open new terminal.
Retrieve web page.
curl 127.0.0.1:8080/index.php
Output:
<html>
<body>
Hello, World!
</body>
</html>
Pod is operational.
________________________________________
17. Add Debugging Page
A debugging script is provided.
File:
phpinfo.php
Purpose:
Display PHP configuration information
________________________________________
18. Copy Debug File to Pod
Copy the file.
oc cp ~/DO180/labs/pods-review/phpinfo.php \
webphp:/var/www/html/phpinfo.php
Destination directory:
/var/www/html
________________________________________
19. View PHP Debug Page
Open browser or curl:
http://127.0.0.1:8080/phpinfo.php
This displays:
PHP configuration
modules
environment variables
server information
Very useful for debugging PHP applications.
________________________________________
20. Stop Port Forwarding
Return to first terminal.
Press:
Ctrl + C
________________________________________
21. Grade the Lab
Check solution.
lab grade pods-review
Fix errors if needed.
________________________________________
22. Finish the Lab
Clean environment.
lab finish pods-review
________________________________________
Key Troubleshooting Workflow
Typical debugging process:
1 oc run pod
2 oc get pods
3 oc get events
4 oc logs
5 oc debug pod
6 fix container image
7 oc edit pod
8 oc port-forward
9 oc cp debug files
________________________________________
Main Issue in This Lab
Root cause:
Permission error in /run/httpd directory
Container runs with:
Random UID
root group
Fix:
Give correct permissions to root group
________________________________________
✅ Final Result
•	Pod successfully running
•	Web server accessible
•	PHP debugging page added

